# ============================================================================
# STAGING frontend environment.  P2A - configuration only; nothing deployed.
# ============================================================================
#
# Every value here is inlined into the client bundle at BUILD time by Vite. That
# is the defining property of this file, and it is the reason it holds so few
# variables: everything prefixed VITE_ is public the moment the site is
# reachable, including in the JS source map.
#
# CONSEQUENCE: a secret that is ever given a VITE_ prefix is a published secret.
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY, DATABASE_URL, REDIS_URL, the Razorpay key SECRET, the
# Razorpay webhook secret and the AI key are SERVER variables. Setting them here
# does not make them private - it makes them world-readable.
#
# Set these in the frontend host's dashboard (Vercel: Project Settings -> Env
# Vars), per environment. Do NOT commit a filled-in copy of this file.
#
# ============================================================================
# 1. WHICH SUPABASE PROJECT
# ============================================================================
#
# This MUST be the same project as the API's SUPABASE_URL. The API verifies the
# `iss` and `aud` of every token against that URL, so a frontend pointed at a
# different project produces the most confusing failure in this stack: sign-in
# SUCCEEDS in the browser, the project issues a perfectly valid token, and then
# every API call returns 401 - which from the server side is indistinguishable
# from a real attack. There is a test for exactly this drift
# (`test_infra_contract.py`), but it can only compare what is committed.
#
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://<STAGING_PROJECT_REF>.supabase.co

# Publishable key, NOT the service_role/secret key. Designed to be public: it
# identifies the project and the browser cannot read anything without a session.
# Read as a VITE_ variable by `src/lib/supabase.ts`, which fails loudly at import
# if it is missing - see the test env config in vitest.config.ts for why that
# matters.
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ============================================================================
# 2. WHERE THE API IS
# ============================================================================
#
# The absolute origin of the STAGING backend, e.g. https://<STAGING_API_HOST>.
# No trailing slash, no /api/v1 - the prefix is applied by `src/lib/api.ts`.
#
# Left EMPTY, the client calls a relative `/api/v1`, which is the local dev
# arrangement where Vite proxies the API (see vite.config.ts). That works in
# `npm run dev` and on a host that reverse-proxies /api to the backend. It does
# NOT work on a plain static host with no proxy, which is why this must be set
# explicitly for staging.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=https://<STAGING_API_HOST>

# ============================================================================
# 3. ENVIRONMENT AND ORIGIN
# ============================================================================
#
# `staging` shows the "open console link" affordance for authentication errors,
# which is deliberately hidden in production. The union type lives in
# `src/vite-env.d.ts`, so a typo here is a type error at build time.
VITE_APP_ENV=staging

# The staging frontend's own origin. Used for absolute links (password reset,
# OAuth return) and must match the origin in the API's CORS_ORIGINS allow-list,
# or the browser blocks the response.
VITE_SITE_URL=https://<STAGING_FRONTEND_HOST>

# Shown to users who need support. A staging mailbox, never a real person's.
VITE_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support-staging@example.invalid

# ============================================================================
# NOT SET HERE
# ============================================================================
#
#   SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY     server-side; mints signed URLs, has BYPASSRLS
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY   the same key under its legacy name
#   DATABASE_URL            server-side
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL     server-side, Alembic only
#   REDIS_URL               server-side
#   RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET     server-side
#   RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET server-side
#   AI_PROVIDER_API_KEY     server-side
#   CORS_ORIGINS            server-side; the frontend does not send it
#
# The public half of a pair is safe to publish, the secret half never is. The
# only one of these with a legitimate browser-side counterpart is
# RAZORPAY_KEY_ID, and the backend supplies that to the checkout payload itself -
# so the frontend still does not need to hold it.
